  "body": {
    "id": "lem:gap-future-measurability",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(s\\in\\mathcal S\\), the counterfactual statistic\n\\[\n T_{s,n}^{\\mathrm{gap}}\n =\\frac{\\widehat\\tau_{B,C,s}-\\tau}\n        {\\widehat{\\operatorname{se}}_s}\n\\]\nand every fitted object entering it are measurable with respect to \\(\\sigma(X_t:t\\in\\mathcal B_n\\cup\\mathcal C_n)\\).  The selected statistic equals \\(T_{\\widehat J_A,n}^{\\mathrm{gap}}\\), while \\(\\widehat J_A\\) is \\(\\mathcal F_A\\)-measurable.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "s",
      "S",
      "tauhatBCS",
      "tau",
      "sehats",
      "Bn",
      "Cn",
      "JhatA",
      "FA"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:gap-split-estimator"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The sets \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) and \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) are deterministic.  For fixed \\(s\\), \\(\\widehat M_{B,s}\\), its invertibility indicator, the inverse on that event, the fixed zero fallback, and \\(\\widehat\\alpha_{B,s}\\) are Borel functions of \\((X_t:t\\in\\mathcal B_n)\\).  The vector \\(\\widehat\\mu_{C,s}\\) is a Borel function of \\((X_t:t\\in\\mathcal C_n)\\).  Hence \\(\\widehat H_{B,C,s}\\), including its fixed fallback, is measurable with respect to \\(\\sigma(X_t:t\\in\\mathcal B_n\\cup\\mathcal C_n)\\).  The displayed finite sums defining \\(\\widehat u_{B,s,t}\\), \\(\\widehat\\psi_{B,s,t}\\), \\(\\widehat\\tau_{B,C,s}\\), \\(\\widehat v_{C,s,t}\\), their sample means and lag products, the two Bartlett estimates, and the piecewise square-root fallback are compositions of Borel maps of the same future observations.  Thus \\(T_{s,n}^{\\mathrm{gap}}\\) is future-measurable.  Finally \\(\\widehat J_A\\) is defined from the observations generating \\(\\mathcal F_A\\), and substitution into the fixed-role formula gives \\(T_{\\widehat J_A,n}^{\\mathrm{gap}}\\).",
